# /{{VERB_REFINE}} — Idea → agent-ready ({{ORG_NAME}})

Refine is the **first half** of the flow. A ticket leaves refine only when it is
*agent-ready*: an agent could pick it up and execute without guessing. The bar is set
by the wiki's operating model — read it (via `/prime`) before refining.

> **Why this matters:** agents execute well-specified work, not half-baked ideas. If
> refinement is thin, execution produces ambiguous tasks and the harness escalates
> back to you. Refine well once; execute cleanly after.

## Invocation

```
/refine <ticket-key>
```

## Prerequisites

- `/prime <ticket-key>` has run this session (you know the current flow + rules).
- The operating model's **refine** definition is loaded — it is the authority on
  what "agent-ready" means for {{ORG_NAME}} and may have changed since last release.

## What this skill does

Work **conversationally with the engineer**, one decision at a time. Pull context
at T1 first; promote to detail only when a specific decision needs it.

### 1. Load the ticket at T1

{{TRACKER_VIEW_ISSUE_SNIPPET}}

Also read the ticket's comments — prior decisions/refinements are recorded there, not
in the description, and the view above does not include them:

{{TRACKER_COMMENT_LIST_SNIPPET}}

Surface: key, title, the user need / problem statement, current state. Don't pull
the full body until a decision requires it.

### 2. Establish the user need

Restate the problem as a user-facing need ("a *&lt;user&gt;* needs to *&lt;do X&gt;*"). If
the operating model defines a product/engineering split for who owns the need and
the acceptance test, respect it — surface where the engineer should pull in product.

### 3. Co-author the validation / acceptance test (the outer goal)

The definition-of-done as a **test**: the concrete, observable proof the need is met.
This is the outer red the execute phase will drive to green. Per the operating
model, this is typically product-owned and engineering co-authored — flag if
sign-off is needed.

### 4. Place the constraints

Guided by the wiki's baselines (read them; do not invent):

- **SLOs** — always user-facing; only *who the user is* changes. Latency, error
  rate, availability targets for the affected surface.
- **Acceptance criteria** — concrete and testable, each mapped to how it will be proven.
- **Proposed architecture** — in tech terms: which repos are touched, which patterns.
- **Security analysis** — of both problem and solution surface.
- **Design controls** — if the touched repo is under design controls, note the
  requirement→spec→test traceability the work must produce (the gate validates it;
  the wiki's design-controls ADR is the authority on where those records live).

### 5. Map dependencies + rollout

- Cross-repo / cross-team / external-API dependencies and deployment ordering.
- Rollout: feature-flag plan, canary criteria, rollback triggers.
- Analytics instrumentation: which events prove the journey and feed the SLO dashboards.

### 6. Write artifacts back to the tracker

Persist refinement to the ticket as you go (description sections or fields) — the
tracker is the system of record, not this session. Keep the session lean.

{{TRACKER_COMMENT_SNIPPET}}

### 7. Check agent-readiness (optional, on demand)

Dispatch the **{{CLEARANCE_AGENT}}** agent in `agent-ready` mode to assess gaps against the wiki's
gate rules. It returns a readiness verdict + a deficiency list. Iterate until clean.

**Do not** pass this gate without a defined validation/acceptance test — that test is
the forcing function: it makes the engineer problem-solve up front and it drives ATDD
in `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}`. No test ⇒ not agent-ready, full stop.

### 8. Mark agent-ready

When the gate is satisfied, mark the ticket agent-ready. The gate is carried by a
**board-agnostic label** (`agent-ready`), not a board-specific status — so this works
on any team's Jira board without touching their own workflow. Applying the label *is*
the proof the ticket was refined and has its acceptance test.

{{TRACKER_GATE_SNIPPET}}

From here, `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` takes over (and refuses any ticket missing this label).

## Escalation

Stop and surface to the engineer (do not guess) when: the need is ambiguous, an SLO
baseline is missing from the wiki, the architecture implies an ADR-level decision,
or a regulated surface is touched. Humans are the top of the loop.

## Tool contract

Reads the wiki + tracker + codebase; writes refinement artifacts to the **tracker**.
Does not write to the wiki and does not write code.

## Implementation note

Follow this SKILL.md as a procedure; it is not auto-executed.
